 "youtube": {
  "title": "Bat Echolocation: The Sound Nobody Heard for 145 Years",
  "description": "Bat echolocation was a mystery for 145 years: how do bats fly through a pitch-dark room without touching a single thread?\n\nIn 1793 the Italian priest and scientist Lazzaro Spallanzani watched bats dodge silk threads in total darkness while an owl crashed into the walls. Louis Jurine plugged their ears and the bats were lost, yet bats seemed completely silent. Georges Cuvier said it was touch, and the world believed him for more than a century, until a Harvard student named Donald Griffin held a bat up to an ultrasound detector in 1938.\n\nIn this video:\n- Spallanzani's dark room, the owl and the silk threads\n- The ear test that pointed to hearing\n- Why Cuvier's touch theory won anyway\n- Hiram Maxim, Hamilton Hartridge and the missing machine\n- The 1938 detector and the birth of the word echolocation\n\nSubscribe to Stickstory for more of nature's hidden superpowers. What other sounds do you think are all around us that we can't hear? Tell me in the comments!\n\n#batecholocation #bats #sciencehistory #animalsuperpowers #stickstory",
